## 7. Attendance management

Each attendance event must include:
- Employee ID.
- Event type: check-in or check-out.
- Server-generated timestamp.
- Assigned schedule reference.
- Location verification result.
- GPS coordinates when successfully obtained and policy permits collection.
- Reported location accuracy in meters.
- Applicable GPS policy version.
- Device/browser metadata limited to what is necessary.
- Event source: personal device or shared laboratory device.
- Exception or override reference, if applicable.

Use server time as the authoritative attendance time.

Never accept the employee's device clock as the official timestamp.

Use an atomic server-side transaction or equivalent protection against duplicate attendance events and race conditions.

Attendance state rules:
- A valid check-in opens an attendance session.
- A valid check-out closes the open session.
- A check-out cannot exist without a valid open attendance session, except through an explicitly authorized correction workflow.
- Prevent overlapping sessions.
- Prevent duplicate check-in.
- Do not silently overwrite previous events.
- Support correction requests without destroying original records.

If a user taps a button repeatedly, process only a valid event.

Display confirmation before submission when appropriate.

## 8. GPS location verification and per-employee policies

Managers must be able to configure GPS rules individually for every employee.

Allow configuration of:
- GPS required or optional.
- Authorized laboratory location.
- Latitude and longitude.
- Allowed radius in meters.
- Separate policies for check-in and check-out if needed.
- Behavior for location outside the allowed area.
- Behavior for poor location accuracy.
- Behavior when location services are disabled.
- Exception request availability.
- Policy effective date.
- Authorized manager responsible for exceptions.

Allow different employees to have different policies.

Provide a map-based configuration interface when a suitable mapping provider is configured. If no mapping API is available, support manual coordinates and provide a simple alternative location configuration workflow.

Default configuration:
- GPS required.
- 100-meter radius.
- Configurable per employee.
- Location outside the allowed area triggers a restriction or exception workflow according to policy.
- Poor GPS accuracy triggers a warning or exception workflow.

These defaults must be editable.

GPS requirements:
- Request browser location permission at the moment it is needed.
- Use HTTPS.
- Show the user why location is required.
- Handle permission denial, timeout, unavailable GPS, and inaccurate location.
- Calculate distance between reported coordinates and authorized coordinates on the server.
- Validate coordinate ranges and accuracy values.
- Never treat browser-reported GPS as cryptographically verified proof of physical presence.
- Flag suspicious or inconsistent location patterns where feasible.
- Do not claim that GPS spoofing can be completely prevented.
- Minimize storage of precise location information and restrict access.
- Keep location retention configurable and documented.

For employees who are not allowed to check in/out outside the authorized area:
- Provide a clear Persian error message.
- Provide a request-exception option where permitted.
- Preserve an auditable decision trail.

For GPS-optional employees:
- Permit the event under policy.
- Record that location was not verified.
- Do not falsely mark the location as verified.

Provide a secure, server-validated exception workflow.

Do not automatically trust a location supplied by the client.

